The '''Lamalama''' language, also known by the [[List of Australian Aboriginal group names|clan name]] '''Mbarrumbathama''' ([[Austlang]]) or '''Mba Rumbathama''', formerly known as  '''Lamu-Lamu''' or  '''Lama-Lama''', is a [[Paman languages|Paman language]] of [[Queensland]], [[Australia]]. Lamalama is one of four languages once spoken by the [[Lama Lama people|Lamalama]] people, the others being [[Morrobolam language|Morrobolam]] (Umbuygamu), [[Mbariman-Gudhinma language|Mbariman-Gudinhma]], and [[Umpithamu language|Umpithamu]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Crump |first=Des |date=2020-07-20 |title=Language of the Week: Week Eight - Lamalama |url=https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/language-week-week-eight-lamalama |access-date=2023-12-15 |website=State Library Of Queensland |language=en}}</ref>

==Naming and language relationships==
In January 2019, the ISO database changed its reference name to Lamalama, from Lamu-Lamu.<ref>{{cite web | title=lby | website=ISO 639-3 | date=25 January 2019 | url=https://iso639-3.sil.org/code/lby | access-date=7 August 2020}}</ref> {{as of|August 2020}}, [[Glottolog]] calls it Lamalama,<ref>{{cite web | title= 4.2.1 - Lamalama | website=Glottolog | url=https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/lamu1254 | language=jv | access-date=7 August 2020}}</ref> while [[AIATSIS]]' Austlang database thesaurus heading is Mbarrumbathama language.<ref name=austlang>{{cite web | title=Y136: Mbarrumbathama | website=AIATSIS Collection: AUSTLANG | date=26 July 2019 | url=https://collection.aiatsis.gov.au/austlang/language/y136 | access-date=7 August 2020}}</ref>

Austlang says, quoting linguist Jean-Cristophe Verstraete (2018), that Lamalama, [[Rimanggudinhma language|Rimanggudinhma]] (Mbariman-Gudhinma) and [[Morrobolam language|Morrobolam]] form a [[Genetic relationship (linguistics)|genetic]] subgroup of Paman known as Lamalamic, "defined by shared innovations in phonology and morphology". Within this subgroup, "Morrobolam and Lamalama form a phonologically innovative branch, while Rumanggudinhma forms a more conservative branch".<ref>{{cite web | title=Y55: Morrobolam | website=AIATSIS Collection (Austlang) | date=26 July 2019 | url=https://collection.aiatsis.gov.au/austlang/language/Y55 | access-date=7 August 2020}}</ref>

