* Voiceless fricative sounds {{IPA|/ɸ, θ, ɕ, h/}} are heard as voiced [{{IPA link|β}}, {{IPA link|ð}}, {{IPA link|ʑ}}, {{IPA link|ɦ}}] when in consonant clusters and in intervocalic positions.{{sfn|Verstraete|2019|p=273}}
* Fricatives {{IPA|/θ, ɕ/}} can be heard as laminal and alveolar fricatives [{{IPA link|s̻}}, {{IPA link|s}}] when in word-initial position in free variation among speakers.{{sfn|Verstraete|2019|p=272}}
* {{IPA|/h/}} can be heard as {{IPAblink|x}} when in word-initial position in free variation among speakers.{{sfn|Verstraete|2019|p=272}}
* The fricative trill {{IPAslink|r̝}} is also heard as voiceless {{IPAblink|r̝̥}} in free variation in initial positions.{{sfn|Verstraete|2019|p=275}}
* The trill sound {{IPA|/r/}} can be heard as voiceless {{IPAblink|r̥}} when in word-final contexts.{{sfn|Verstraete|2019|p=276}}
* All labial consonants can also be [[labialization|labialized]] optionally within the onset of stressed syllables, or when after high-back vowel sounds.{{sfn|Verstraete|2019|pp=277-279}}
* Consonant lengthening {{IPA|[Cː]}} can be heard within the onset of stressed syllables.{{sfn|Verstraete|2019|pp=276-277}}

